{"kind":"artifact","text":"The architecture reference distinguishes an affordance from a signature, names both signing boundaries, and stops overstating what the guard covers.\n\nExact clean pushed head b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be on request/signing-standing-repair; tree b6d2c14a4f38890fe08caaf8555cab30273f8a4a, checkout clean, origin matching by commit SHA. Supersedes my candidate e55efbb9 at this path.\n\nWHAT IT NOW SAYS. A new passage under the live-membership boundary states that what a control offers and what may be signed are different questions with different answers, because authority moves under an open form — a lease expires, a grant is superseded, a target is retired — and the fold judges the record by what is true when it arrives. It names the guard and both boundaries that ask it, doAct in App.tsx and send in Thread.tsx, and records that publishing asks publishRefusal at its own boundary instead. It then states the ratify rule in the fold's own order: standing first, a target not ruled effective and a retired target both refused before any satisfier is consulted, and only then who may satisfy the kind. It says standing fails closed and why, and it says effectiveness is published per record in decisions rather than carried on the statement, which is the fact that forces the boundary to be handed the decision as well as the record. It closes on the cost: the harm is not a failed click but a permanent ineffective row in an append-only log.\n\nWHY IT RESTS ON THE BEHAVIOUR ARTIFACTS. This page describes behaviour, so it rests on the ui artifact 056b5348 and the internal/workroom artifact 149fd3c9 at this same head, not only on the promise. Resting a documentation artifact on its promise alone means no succession can ever flare it: the world it describes can move underneath and the page goes on asserting a settled architecture it no longer matches. The two bases named are exactly the layers whose contract this passage states — the browser guard and the fold rule it must agree with — so when either is superseded, this page is told.\n\nA CORRECTION, NOT A SILENT EDIT. The claim that every durable browser act passes through signingRefusal was false, and the false version was mine. Publishing never went through it. The page and the source comment both say so now, and both say it as a correction rather than by quietly dropping the sentence, because a guard that overstates its own reach is precisely how the next reader stops checking — which is how the standing defect survived a review in the first place.\n\nCONDITIONS MET. The contract change is published in the same head as the code that makes it true, which is what step 4 of AGENTS.md requires of a review that changes a layer's contract.\n\nGates at this exact head: gofmt clean, go build, go vet, go test ./... all pass, and internal/docset with -count=1 passes, which matters here because the docset gate caches past .md edits and a cached pass would predate this change.","body":{"commit":"b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}